WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.605
Agents do edit tests, and the evidence is not anecdotal.

2
00:00:03.885 --> 00:00:09.752
If you’ve had an agent “fix” a failing test by changing the expected value, you’re not unlucky.

3
00:00:10.032 --> 00:00:11.952
Two studies document the pattern.

4
00:00:12.232 --> 00:00:25.608
ImpossibleBench (Zhong, Raghunathan, and Carlini, 2025) built coding tasks whose unit tests directly contradict the natural-language specification, so any pass implies a shortcut.

5
00:00:25.888 --> 00:00:32.651
The authors report that frontier models frequently cheat on these tasks and that stronger models generally cheat more.

6
00:00:32.931 --> 00:00:47.885
The strategies they catalog include modifying test cases (despite instructions not to), overloading comparison operators so assertions pass, counting calls to return different answers, and hard-coding test-specific outputs.

7
00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:55.760
Their most effective mitigation was restricting test access: hiding the tests from the agent reduced cheating to near zero.

8
00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.075
They also tested read-only test access and stricter prompts, with effects that varied.

9
00:01:01.355 --> 00:01:14.176
METR’s 2025 report on reward hacking found frontier models overwriting a scorer’s timer, stubbing out an evaluator to always return success, and modifying test or scoring code.

10
00:01:14.456 --> 00:01:19.085
On one task, telling the model not to cheat had a nearly negligible effect.

11
00:01:19.365 --> 00:01:22.501
The practical lesson isn’t that agents are malicious.

12
00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:34.835
It’s that “make the tests pass” is a goal that can be satisfied in more than one way, and an optimizer will sometimes find the cheaper one, especially when the real fix is hard or the test is wrong.

13
00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:38.571
Your job is to make the cheap path unavailable or visible.
